Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Your bench press is surging forward — keep this momentum and you'll hit 100 kg.",
  "summary": "This was a strong week across the board. Your chest strength index jumped from 106 to 112, the biggest single-period gain of any muscle group, which is excellent news given your goal of benching 100 kg. Triceps also ticked up nicely from 105 to 108, providing important support for your pressing work. Total tonnage of over 10,500 kg at your bodyweight reflects a solid, productive training load for a 28-year-old male at your size and experience level. The only flag this week is a small dip in back strength (101 to 96), which is worth monitoring but not alarming on its own. Overall, this period delivered meaningful progress toward your long-term goal.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "status": "on_track",
    "notes": "Chest strength index rose from 106 to 112, a strong 5.7% gain in a single period, directly supporting the 100 kg bench press goal. Triceps followed suit, climbing from 105 to 108, which is encouraging since triceps strength is a key limiting factor in heavy pressing. Shoulders held flat at 104 and legs and biceps were unchanged at 102 and 100 respectively, suggesting maintenance rather than growth in those areas this period. The back index slipped from 101 to 96, which is the one area that bucked the positive trend and should be watched next period to determine if it is a one-off or the start of a regression."
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
